turns.assistant:
Use an `AbortController` so the first request is cancelled when React unmounts the component:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();

  fetch('/api/metrics', { signal: controller.signal })
    .then(res => res.json())
    .then(data => setMetrics(data))
    .catch(err => {
      if (err.name !== 'AbortError') setError(err);
    });

  return () => controller.abort();
}, []);
```

In dev you'll still see two requests in the network tab, but the first one shows as cancelled and won't update state. In production there's just one.

That error means `document.getElementById(...)` is returning `null`, so the ID you're passing doesn't match the element in your HTML.

Check:
1. In `index.html`, find the root div, e.g. `<div id="root"></div>` or `<div id="app"></div>`.
2. In `main.tsx`, make sure you're using the same ID:

```tsx
import { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client';

const container = document.getElementById('root');
const root = createRoot(container!);
root.render(
  <React.StrictMode>
    <App />
  </React.StrictMode>
);
```

The old `ReactDOM.render` call probably had the correct ID; copy it from there.
